NAME
       pvs - list physical volumes, one line each

SYNOPSIS
       pvs [-a] [-o FIELDS] [-O SORT] [-S SELECTION] [--units U] [--noheadings]
           [--nosuffix] [--separator S] [PV...]

DESCRIPTION
       pvs prints a table of the physical volumes (PVs) LVM knows: the device,
       the volume group it belongs to (empty when it is in none yet), the
       format (lvm2), attributes, its size and how much of it is still free.
       Use it to check that pvcreate worked and which disks a VG uses. It
       changes nothing. Run it as root.

       In the Attr column, a means the PV can take new extents (allocatable)
       and u that it is used by a VG.

OPTIONS
       -o, --options FIELDS
              Show these columns; a leading + adds them to the defaults.
              Fields include pv_name, vg_name, pv_size, pv_free, pv_used,
              pv_attr, pv_uuid, pv_pe_count, pv_pe_alloc_count, dev_size.

       -O, --sort FIELDS
              Sort by these fields.

       -S, --select EXPRESSION
              Show only matching rows, such as -S vg_name=vgdata.

       -a, --all
              Also list devices that are not PVs.

       --units U
              Sizes in one unit: b, k, m, g, t, or h (readable, the default).

       --noheadings
              Leave out the header line.

       --nosuffix
              Leave the unit letter off sizes.

       --separator S
              Separate the columns with S.

EXAMPLES
       List the PVs:

           pvs

       Show only the free space on /dev/vdb1, in MiB:

           pvs --noheadings --units m -o pv_free /dev/vdb1

SEE ALSO
       pvcreate(8), pvdisplay(8), vgs(8), lvs(8), lvm(8)
